NAME
       malloc, calloc, realloc, free - allocate memory

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void *malloc(size_t size);
       void *calloc(size_t n, size_t size);
       void *realloc(void *p, size_t size);
       void  free(void *p);

DESCRIPTION
       The heap is a list of blocks carrying their own size, grown with
       sbrk in 64 KB steps. malloc takes the first block large enough
       and splits it; free marks a block available and merges it with
       neighbours that are also free.

       calloc zeroes what it returns. realloc keeps the contents up to
       the smaller of the two sizes and may move the block, so the old
       pointer must not be used afterwards.

RETURN VALUE
       A pointer, or NULL with errno set to ENOMEM. free returns
       nothing and accepts NULL.

NOTES
       Large allocations are fine: the break has no small ceiling, and
       decoding a long animated GIF into a dozen megabytes of frames
       works.

SEE ALSO
       brk(2), mmap(2)
